<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Br. J. Cancer</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">10027313</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2362434</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6690071</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.bjc.6690071</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Regular Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Constitutional genetic variation at the human aromatase gene (<italic>Cyp19</italic>) and breast cancer risk</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Siegelmann-Danieli</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><xref rid="note1" ref-type="fn"/><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Buetow</surname><given-names>K H</given-names></name><xref rid="note2" ref-type="fn"/><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1">Division of Population Science, Fox Chase Cancer Center, Philadelphia, PA, USA</aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="caf1"><label>*</label>Department of Oncology, Rambam Medical Center, PO Box 9602, Haifa 31096, Israel</corresp><fn fn-type="present-address" id="note1"><p>Authors' current affiliations: Department of Oncology, Rambam Medical Center, PO Box 9602, Haifa 31096, Israel;</p></fn><fn fn-type="present-address" id="note2"><p>Fox Chase Cancer Center, 7701 Burlhome Avenue, Philadelphia, PA 19111, USA.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>2</month><year>1999</year></pub-date><volume>79</volume><issue>3-4</issue><fpage>456</fpage><lpage>463</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>15</day><month>12</month><year>1997</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>21</day><month>04</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>11</day><month>03</month><year>1998</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1999</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>The activity of the aromatase enzyme, which converts androgens into oestrogens and has a major role in regulating oestrogen levels in the breast, is thought to be a contributing factor in the development of breast cancer. We undertook this study to assess the role of constitutional genetic variation in the human aromatase gene (<italic>Cyp19</italic>) in the development of this disease. Our genotyping of 348 cases with breast cancer and 145 controls (all Caucasian women) for a published tetranucleotide repeat polymorphism at intron 4 of the <italic>Cyp19</italic> gene revealed the presence of six common and two rare alleles. Contingency table analysis revealed a significant difference in allelic distribution between cases and controls (&#x003c7;<sup>2</sup> 5df = 13.52, <italic>P</italic> = 0.019). The allele measuring 171 bp was over-represented in cases; of 14 individuals homozygous for this allele, 13 were cases. These individuals had a higher incidence of cancer in family members and an earlier age at diagnosis than other cases. In sequencing <italic>Cyp19</italic>'s coding exons and regulatory regions, we discovered a perfect association between a silent polymorphism (G&#x02192;A at Val80) and the high-risk genotype. Our conclusion is that constitutional genetic variation at the <italic>Cyp19</italic> locus is associated with the risk of developing breast cancer, with the 171-bp allele serving as the high-risk allele. &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>breast cancer</kwd><kwd>aromatase gene</kwd><kwd>oestrogens</kwd><kwd><italic>Cyp19</italic></kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>